<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dutch Water Line</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1672">1672</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Holland Water Line, Hollandic Water Line</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Water Line was a defensive line of fortified towns and low land that could be deliberately flooded, protecting the heartland of Holland on its eastern side. First used on a large scale in 1672, when it halted the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> short of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, it was later extended eastwards as the New Dutch Water Line in the nineteenth century, which is now part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dutch-water-line.jpg" alt="Dutch Water Line" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J. Hoynck van Papendrecht · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Willem_III_inspecteert_Waterlinie,_1672.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Early in the Eighty Years' War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> learnt that flooding low-lying land was an excellent defence, as the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> showed in 1574. Once Holland was free of Spanish troops, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange">Maurice of Nassau</a> planned to defend it with a line of flooded land guarded by fortresses, running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuiderzee">Zuiderzee</a> down to the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waal">Waal</a>. His half-brother Prince Frederick Henry began the work in 1629, building sluices in the dykes and forts and fortified towns at strategic points, with guns covering the dykes that crossed the line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The water was kept deep enough to make an advance on foot precarious and shallow enough to rule out boats other than the flat-bottomed gun barges of the defenders. Ditches and trous de loup were hidden below the surface, the trees along the dykes could be felled as abatis, and in winter the level could be changed to weaken the ice. Frost was the line's weakness: freezing nearly made it useless in 1672, and in 1794 and 1795 the revolutionary French armies crossed it only because the floods had frozen solid.</p><p>After 1815 King William I of the United Kingdom of the Netherlands extended the line east to include Utrecht, and in the 19th century it gained forts with round gun towers like Martello towers. It was mobilised but never attacked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> of 1870 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> many pillboxes were added, the most numerous being 570 unarmed structures of the Type P Shelter, but the army chose the Grebbe Line as its main defence. In 1940 German airborne troops seized the bridges at Moerdijk, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dordrecht">Dordrecht</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a>, and the bombing of Rotterdam forced the surrender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the war a third line on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijssel">IJssel</a> was planned against a Soviet invasion, to be flooded by diverting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waal">Waal</a>; it was never tested and was dismantled in 1964. From 1815 to 1940 around 50 billion euro in today's terms was spent on the New Dutch Water Line. Many of its forts survive, some used for overnight stays or, like Fort Hoofddijk with Utrecht University's botanical garden, for other purposes. On 26 July 2021 the line joined the Defence Line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> as the Dutch Water Defence Lines World Heritage Site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dutch Waterline” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1672 France, England, Münster and Cologne attacked together, and the Republic survived by flooding the <b>Holland Water Line</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Military</a></li></ul></section>
